---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import FeatureRow from "../components/FeatureRow.astro";
import Shot from "../components/Shot.astro";
---

<Base og="activities"
  title="Activities and sports"
  description="Every COROS session in one list, and a screen each for running, cycling, hiking and strength that reads what that sport is about."
>
  <section class="wrap page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Every session</p>
    <h1>Every session, read in depth.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      One list holds everything your watch has recorded. Running, Cycling, Hiking and Strength
      each get a screen of their own that reads what that sport is about, and every session a
      page with every channel the watch recorded.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section-tight">
    <Shot name="02-activities" alt="Activities: every session in one list with a detail pane" eager />
  </section>

  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="feature-text">
      <p class="eyebrow">Activities</p>
      <h2>Every sport in one list.</h2>
      <ul class="ticks columns">
        <li><strong>Everything COROS holds</strong>, swims and hybrid sessions included, grouped by week with the week's totals.</li>
        <li><strong>Filter</strong> by period, by sport or by name.</li>
        <li><strong>Compare sports</strong> on one bar: how your time splits between them.</li>
        <li><strong>A detail pane</strong> beside the list: zones, laps, running dynamics, power and every channel, and a link to the session's own sport page.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <FeatureRow id="running" shot="03-running" alt="Running: weekly volume, load ratio, VO2max and threshold pace">
      <p class="eyebrow">Running</p>
      <h2>Pace, load and form.</h2>
      <p>
        Weekly volume against its four-week average, your load ratio, VO₂max and threshold pace,
        how much of your running is easy, and a split by surface.
      </p>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li><strong>Trail runs read as trail runs.</strong> Switch to Trail and the screen counts hours and climb instead of pace, with climb per hour as the measure of progress.</li>
        <li><strong>Running efficiency</strong> across the period: speed for the heart rate it cost, on your COROS VO₂max scale.</li>
      </ul>
    </FeatureRow>

    <FeatureRow shot="04-run" alt="A run: map, pace, heart rate, running form and every channel" flip>
      <p class="eyebrow">A run's page</p>
      <h2>Every channel the watch recorded.</h2>
      <p>
        The route on a map, then pace, effort pace, heart rate, cadence, power, stride, ground
        time and stride height on one chart in COROS's own colours, read a stretch at a time or
        split in two: what you produced above, what it cost below.
      </p>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li><strong>Running form</strong> and the session's effect, weather and conditions.</li>
        <li><strong>Time in every zone</strong> as COROS scored it, and a label for what the session trained.</li>
        <li><strong>Laps</strong> headed by the workout's parts, with the recoveries marked Rest.</li>
      </ul>
    </FeatureRow>

    <FeatureRow shot="27-run-efficiency" alt="Running efficiency: a long run against the athlete's usual, minute by minute">
      <p class="eyebrow">Running efficiency</p>
      <h2>Speed for the heartbeats it cost.</h2>
      <p>
        How much speed each minute of a road or track run bought for the heart rate it cost, on
        your COROS VO₂max scale and against your usual from the six weeks before.
      </p>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li><strong>Durability</strong>: how well you held it to the end, against how much you usually fade.</li>
        <li><strong>Read fairly</strong>: the warm-up, surges and stops are marked rather than counted.</li>
      </ul>
    </FeatureRow>

    <FeatureRow id="cycling" shot="25-cycling" alt="Cycling: hours this week, load ratio, FTP and climb">
      <p class="eyebrow">Cycling</p>
      <h2>Hours, power and climb.</h2>
      <p>
        A week of riding is read in hours, because an indoor ride may record no distance and an
        hour off-road covers half the road's. FTP and watts per kilo, the week's climb, and a
        split by road, gravel, mountain, indoor and e-bike.
      </p>
    </FeatureRow>

    <FeatureRow shot="05-ride" alt="A ride: normalised power, intensity, TSS and peak power" flip>
      <p class="eyebrow">A ride's page</p>
      <h2>The power behind a ride.</h2>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li><strong>Normalised power, IF and TSS</strong> against your FTP, variability and work.</li>
        <li><strong>Peak power</strong> from 5 seconds to an hour.</li>
        <li><strong>Time in your COROS power zones</strong>, with coasting set apart.</li>
        <li><strong>The climbs</strong>, found in the elevation and categorised by length and grade.</li>
        <li><strong>Speed</strong> over the time you were moving, and a top speed held for five seconds, so one GPS jump does not count.</li>
      </ul>
    </FeatureRow>

    <FeatureRow id="hiking" shot="26-hiking" alt="Hiking: time, ascent, biggest day and ascent per hour">
      <p class="eyebrow">Hiking</p>
      <h2>Height and hours before distance.</h2>
      <p>
        Hikes and mountain climbs, read the way a walker thinks about them: this week's ascent,
        your biggest day, and ascent per hour as the measure of how fit you are on a hill.
      </p>
    </FeatureRow>

    <FeatureRow shot="06-hike" alt="A hike: moving time, ascent, terrain and climbing rate" flip>
      <p class="eyebrow">A hike's page</p>
      <h2>A long day, taken apart.</h2>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li><strong>Moving time</strong> worked out from the samples, because a watch records the lunch stop too.</li>
        <li><strong>Every rest</strong> of two minutes or more, with the time, the kilometre and the altitude.</li>
        <li><strong>Terrain</strong>: time climbing, on the flat and descending, and your speed on each.</li>
        <li><strong>Ascents and descents</strong>, the highest point, and your time against Naismith's rule.</li>
      </ul>
    </FeatureRow>

    <FeatureRow id="strength" shot="07-strength" alt="Strength: sessions and a 3D muscle map">
      <p class="eyebrow">Strength</p>
      <h2>Every set, on the muscles it worked.</h2>
      <p>
        COROS strength sessions and your Hevy workouts in one place, on a 3D map of the muscles
        you trained, with the sets and the weight each muscle took and your coverage of each
        muscle group.
      </p>
      <p>
        COROS scores a session by heart rate, so an hour of heavy squats can count for less than a
        short jog. Choose <strong>Heracles</strong> under <strong>Training load formula</strong> in
        the Strength screen's Settings and each session is scored from its sets, your body weight and what you
        have lifted before, on the same scale as your runs. Overview, the Calendar, the Training
        Library and Activities then count strength that way, with COROS's figure beside it.
      </p>
      <p>
        COROS's exercise data is often wrong: a squat filed as hamstring work, battle ropes as
        biceps, every twist and side plank with no obliques. That skews your totals and gives
        Coach a muddled picture, so the app describes every movement again, its muscles and
        equipment one by one. Where it disagrees with COROS, that is on purpose. If you think it
        got one wrong, say so in <a href="https://github.com/quochungse/HeraclesRecords/discussions">Discussions</a>
        or <a href="https://github.com/quochungse/HeraclesRecords/issues/new">Issues</a>.
      </p>
      <a class="more" href="/guide/hevy">Connect Hevy</a>
    </FeatureRow>

    <FeatureRow shot="16-route" alt="A hike replayed on a satellite map, coloured by elevation" flip>
      <p class="eyebrow">Every route</p>
      <h2>Replayed on the map.</h2>
      <p>
        Open any route full size on a street, satellite or topographic map. Colour the line by
        pace, heart rate or elevation, then watch it draw itself the way you covered it.
      </p>
    </FeatureRow>
  </div>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2 class="section-title">Ask about any session.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Every session's page has <strong>Ask Coach</strong>. The question arrives in Coach
        already pointing at that session, and the answer comes with the figures and charts
        behind it.
      </p>
      <a class="more" href="/coach">How Coach works</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .section-tight {
    padding-top: 40px;
  }

  .feature-text {
    padding-top: 56px;
  }

  .feature-text h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font: 600 clamp(26px, 3.4vw, 32px) / 1.2 var(--font-title);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .columns {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0 32px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .columns {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
